<section xml:id="sec-mod3-worked-examples">
  <title>Module 3: Worked Examples</title>
  <p>Annotated Proof Walkthroughs &#x2014; Estimated time: 40 minutes</p>

    <p>
      Read each example slowly, attending to the proof strategy and
      the annotations as much as to the algebra. The goal is to
      internalize templates that you will reproduce in varied form
      on the practice set and the assessment.
    </p>
    <p>
      The five examples cover the two main proof techniques of this
      module: <em>element-chasing</em> (for set equalities) and
      <em>definition-unpacking</em> (for injectivity, surjectivity,
      and countability). Examples 4 and 5 show that the same
      definition-unpacking approach applies to infinite sets, where
      the conclusion is far less obvious.
    </p>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 1: ELEMENT-CHASING SET EQUALITY
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-we-1">
    <title>Example 1: Element-Chasing a Set Equality</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove: for all sets <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>,
        <md>A \cap (B \cup C) = (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C).</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        To prove two sets are equal, we use the Axiom of Extensionality:
        show each set is a subset of the other. We argue in both
        directions by picking an arbitrary element of each side and
        chasing it through the definitions.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Direction 1: <m>A \cap (B \cup C) \subseteq (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C)</m>.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>x \in A \cap (B \cup C)</m>. By definition of intersection:
        <m>x \in A</m> and <m>x \in B \cup C</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        By definition of union, <m>x \in B \cup C</m> means <m>x \in B</m>
        or <m>x \in C</m>. We consider the two cases separately.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 1:</em> <m>x \in B</m>. Then <m>x \in A</m> (from above)
        and <m>x \in B</m>, so <m>x \in A \cap B</m>. Therefore
        <m>x \in (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 2:</em> <m>x \in C</m>. Then <m>x \in A</m> and
        <m>x \in C</m>, so <m>x \in A \cap C</m>. Therefore
        <m>x \in (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In both cases, <m>x \in (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C)</m>.
        Since <m>x</m> was arbitrary, <m>A \cap (B \cup C) \subseteq (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Direction 2: <m>(A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C) \subseteq A \cap (B \cup C)</m>.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>x \in (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C)</m>. By definition
        of union, either <m>x \in A \cap B</m> or <m>x \in A \cap C</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 1:</em> <m>x \in A \cap B</m>. Then <m>x \in A</m>
        and <m>x \in B</m>. Since <m>x \in B</m>, we have
        <m>x \in B \cup C</m>. So <m>x \in A</m> and
        <m>x \in B \cup C</m>, giving <m>x \in A \cap (B \cup C)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Case 2:</em> <m>x \in A \cap C</m>. Then <m>x \in A</m>
        and <m>x \in C</m>. Since <m>x \in C</m>, we have
        <m>x \in B \cup C</m>. So <m>x \in A \cap (B \cup C)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In both cases, <m>x \in A \cap (B \cup C)</m>.
        Since <m>x</m> was arbitrary,
        <m>(A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C) \subseteq A \cap (B \cup C)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        By both directions, <m>A \cap (B \cup C) = (A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C)</m>.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        Every set equality proof follows this template: two subset
        arguments, each starting with <q>let <m>x \in \ldots</m></q>
        and ending with <q>therefore <m>x \in \ldots</m></q>. The only
        thing that changes from problem to problem is which definitions
        you unpack and which cases you consider. When the union or
        intersection of three or more sets appears, a case split on the
        <q>or</q> in the union definition is almost always the right move.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 2: PROVING BIJECTIVITY AND FINDING THE INVERSE
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-we-2">
    <title>Example 2: Proving Bijectivity and Finding the Inverse</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f : \R \to \R</m> be defined by <m>f(x) = 3x - 5</m>.
        Prove that <m>f</m> is bijective. Then find <m>f^{-1}</m>
        explicitly.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 1: Prove injectivity.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>f(x_1) = f(x_2)</m>. We must show <m>x_1 = x_2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <m>f(x_1) = f(x_2)</m> means <m>3x_1 - 5 = 3x_2 - 5</m>.
        Adding 5 to both sides gives <m>3x_1 = 3x_2</m>, and dividing
        by 3 gives <m>x_1 = x_2</m>. So <m>f</m> is injective.
        <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 2: Prove surjectivity.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        We must show that for every <m>y \in \R</m>, there exists
        <m>x \in \R</m> such that <m>f(x) = y</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Scratch work:</em> Setting <m>3x - 5 = y</m> and solving
        for <m>x</m> gives <m>x = (y + 5)/3</m>. This is a real number
        for any <m>y \in \R</m>, so it is a valid preimage.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Formal proof:</em> Let <m>y \in \R</m>. Define
        <m>x = (y + 5)/3</m>. Then <m>x \in \R</m> (since <m>\R</m>
        is closed under addition and division by nonzero reals), and
        <md>f(x) = 3 \cdot \frac{y+5}{3} - 5 = (y + 5) - 5 = y.</md>
        So <m>f(x) = y</m> as required. Since <m>y</m> was arbitrary,
        <m>f</m> is surjective. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Step 3: Bijectivity and the inverse.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since <m>f</m> is both injective and surjective, it is bijective.
        By Definition 1.11 (Zorn), an inverse function <m>f^{-1} : \R \to \R</m>
        exists. The scratch work in Step 2 gives us its formula:
        <md>f^{-1}(y) = \frac{y + 5}{3}.</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Verification:</em>
        <md>f(f^{-1}(y)) = f\!\left(\frac{y+5}{3}\right) = 3 \cdot \frac{y+5}{3} - 5 = y. \checkmark</md>
        <md>f^{-1}(f(x)) = f^{-1}(3x-5) = \frac{(3x-5)+5}{3} = \frac{3x}{3} = x. \checkmark</md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The proof of surjectivity always has two parts: scratch work
        to find the preimage, and a formal verification that the
        preimage actually works. Do not skip the formal verification;
        in more complex examples the scratch work may suggest a
        candidate that fails a domain check. Also note that
        solving <m>y = f(x)</m> for <m>x</m> is exactly how you
        find <m>f^{-1}</m>&#x2014;this is not a coincidence.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 3: COMPOSITION PRESERVES INJECTIVITY
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-we-3">
    <title>Example 3: Composition Preserves Injectivity</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f : A \to B</m> and <m>g : B \to C</m>. Prove: if
        <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> are both injective, then <m>g \circ f</m>
        is injective.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Strategy.</alert>
        To prove <m>g \circ f</m> is injective, we must show: if
        <m>(g \circ f)(a_1) = (g \circ f)(a_2)</m>, then
        <m>a_1 = a_2</m>. We will use the injectivity of
        <m>g</m> first, then the injectivity of <m>f</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>(g \circ f)(a_1) = (g \circ f)(a_2)</m>.
        By definition of composition, this means
        <m>g(f(a_1)) = g(f(a_2))</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since <m>g</m> is injective, <m>g(f(a_1)) = g(f(a_2))</m>
        implies <m>f(a_1) = f(a_2)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since <m>f</m> is injective, <m>f(a_1) = f(a_2)</m> implies
        <m>a_1 = a_2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Therefore <m>g \circ f</m> is injective. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The proof has a clean three-step structure: unpack composition,
        apply injectivity of <m>g</m>, apply injectivity of <m>f</m>.
        The order matters: <m>g</m> acts on the outputs of <m>f</m>,
        so we peel back the outer function first. This <q>outside-in</q>
        order is the right reflex for any composition proof.
      </p>
      <p>
        An important partial converse is also true: if <m>g \circ f</m>
        is injective, then <m>f</m> must be injective (try proving this
        on the practice set). However, <m>g</m> need not be injective;
        the practice set asks you to find a counterexample.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 4: THE INTEGERS ARE COUNTABLE
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-we-4">
    <title>Example 4: The Integers Are Countably Infinite</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>\Z</m> is countably infinite by constructing
        an explicit bijection <m>f : \N_0 \to \Z</m>, where
        <m>\N_0 = \{0, 1, 2, 3, \ldots\}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Idea.</alert>
        We need a bijection that visits every integer exactly once.
        The natural approach is to alternate between nonnegative and
        negative integers: <m>0, -1, 1, -2, 2, -3, 3, \ldots</m>
        This is the sequence indexed by <m>0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, \ldots</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Construction.</alert>
        Define <m>f : \N_0 \to \Z</m> by:
        <md>
          f(n) = \begin{cases}
            n/2 \amp \text{if } n \text{ is even,} \\
            -(n+1)/2 \amp \text{if } n \text{ is odd.}
          \end{cases}
        </md>
        Checking the first several values:
        <m>f(0) = 0</m>, <m>f(1) = -1</m>, <m>f(2) = 1</m>,
        <m>f(3) = -2</m>, <m>f(4) = 2</m>, <m>f(5) = -3</m>, <m>f(6) = 3</m>.
        The pattern alternates: even inputs map to nonnegative integers,
        odd inputs map to negative integers.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof of injectivity.</alert>
        Suppose <m>f(m) = f(n)</m>. If both are even, then
        <m>m/2 = n/2</m>, so <m>m = n</m>. If both are odd, then
        <m>-(m+1)/2 = -(n+1)/2</m>, so <m>m = n</m>. If one is even
        and the other odd, then <m>f(m) \geq 0</m> and <m>f(n) \lt 0</m>
        (or vice versa), contradicting <m>f(m) = f(n)</m>. In all cases,
        <m>m = n</m>, so <m>f</m> is injective.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Proof of surjectivity.</alert>
        Let <m>k \in \Z</m>. We exhibit an <m>n \in \N_0</m> with
        <m>f(n) = k</m>:
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>k \geq 0</m>: set <m>n = 2k</m>. Then <m>n</m> is even,
        <m>n \geq 0</m>, and <m>f(n) = 2k/2 = k</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>k \lt 0</m>: set <m>n = -2k - 1</m>. Since <m>k \lt 0</m>,
        we have <m>-2k \geq 2 \gt 0</m>, so <m>n \geq 1 \gt 0</m>.
        Also <m>n = -2k - 1</m> is odd (since <m>-2k</m> is even).
        Then <m>f(n) = -(n+1)/2 = -(-2k - 1 + 1)/2 = -(-2k)/2 = k</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In both cases, <m>f(n) = k</m>, so <m>f</m> is surjective.
        Therefore <m>f</m> is a bijection and <m>\Z \sim \N_0 \sim \N</m>,
        so <m>\Z</m> is countably infinite. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The hardest part of a countability proof is constructing the
        bijection. Once you have a candidate, proving injectivity and
        surjectivity follows by applying the definitions. The construction
        here uses even/odd parity to separate two <q>halves</q> of
        <m>\Z</m> and map each half to a separate piece of <m>\N_0</m>.
        A similar strategy (interleaving two countable sets) appears
        in the proof that <m>\Q</m> is countable.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================
       EXAMPLE 5: ℕ × ℕ IS COUNTABLE
  ============================================================ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mod3-we-5">
    <title>Example 5: <m>\N \times \N</m> Is Countably Infinite</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Prove that <m>\N \times \N</m> is countably infinite.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <alert>Idea: the diagonal enumeration.</alert>
        Arrange the elements of <m>\N \times \N</m> in a two-dimensional
        grid, with rows indexed by the first coordinate and columns by
        the second:
      </p>
      <p>
        Row 1: <m>(1,1),\ (1,2),\ (1,3),\ (1,4), \ldots</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Row 2: <m>(2,1),\ (2,2),\ (2,3),\ (2,4), \ldots</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        Row 3: <m>(3,1),\ (3,2),\ (3,3),\ (3,4), \ldots</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        &#x22ee;
      </p>
      <p>
        We traverse the grid along diagonals where the sum
        <m>i + j</m> is constant. Along the diagonal with <m>i + j = 2</m>:
        <m>(1,1)</m>. Along <m>i + j = 3</m>: <m>(1,2), (2,1)</m>.
        Along <m>i + j = 4</m>: <m>(1,3), (2,2), (3,1)</m>. And so on.
        Traversing each finite diagonal in order gives a sequence that
        visits every pair <m>(i,j) \in \N \times \N</m> exactly once.
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Formal argument.</alert>
        The number of pairs with <m>i + j = k</m> (for <m>k \geq 2</m>)
        is <m>k - 1</m>. The total number of pairs with <m>i + j \leq k</m> is
        <m>\sum_{m=2}^{k}(m-1) = \sum_{m=1}^{k-1} m = k(k-1)/2</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Define <m>f : \N \times \N \to \N</m> by:
        <md>f(i,j) = \frac{(i+j-1)(i+j-2)}{2} + i.</md>
        This formula assigns to <m>(i,j)</m> its position in the diagonal
        enumeration: the first term counts how many pairs appear on
        earlier diagonals (diagonals with sum <m>\lt i+j</m>), and the
        second term <m>i</m> gives the position of <m>(i,j)</m> within
        its diagonal (where pairs are ordered by increasing first coordinate).
      </p>
      <p>
        It can be verified that <m>f</m> is a bijection
        <m>\N \times \N \to \N</m>. We verify the first few values:
        <m>f(1,1) = 0 \cdot 0/2 + 1 = 1</m>,
        <m>f(1,2) = 1 \cdot 2/2 + 1 = 2</m>,
        <m>f(2,1) = 1 \cdot 2/2 + 2 = 3</m>,
        <m>f(1,3) = 2 \cdot 3/2 + 1 = 4</m>,
        <m>f(2,2) = 2 \cdot 3/2 + 2 = 5</m>,
        <m>f(3,1) = 2 \cdot 3/2 + 3 = 6</m>.
        This matches the diagonal order. Since <m>f</m> is a bijection,
        <m>\N \times \N \sim \N</m>, so <m>\N \times \N</m> is countably
        infinite. <m>\square</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>What to notice.</alert>
        The bijection formula is worth remembering as a technique, but
        the key insight is the <em>geometric picture</em>: every infinite
        grid can be enumerated by walking along its diagonals. This picture
        underlies the proof of Proposition 1.18 (Zorn) and Corollary 1.20
        (<m>\Q</m> countable). When you need to show that a product or
        union of countable sets is countable, the diagonal picture is the
        right mental model, even if the formal proof uses the abstract
        propositions rather than re-deriving the bijection formula.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</section>
